\mathcal{D} is the quarter circle of radius 3 in the first quadrant.
r = 3
It is described by the inequalities 0\le \theta \le \frac{\pi }{2},\quad 0\le r\le 3 We write f in polar coordinates: f( x,y )=14 xy=14( r \cos \theta )( r \sin \theta )=14 r^2 \cos \theta \sin \theta =7{r}^2 \sin 2\theta Using change of variables in polar coordinates gives \begin{array}{rcl} \int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{D}} 14 xy \,dA \amp =\amp \int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^{3}\left(7{r}^2 \sin 2\theta\right)r\,dr\,d\theta\\ \amp =\amp \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^{3}7{r}^3 \sin 2\theta \,dr \,d\theta\\ \amp =\amp \int_0^{\pi/2} 7\cdot \frac{{r}^4}{4} \sin 2\theta \bigg|_{r=0}^{3}\,d\theta\\ \amp =\amp \int_0^{\pi/2} 7\cdot\frac{3^4}{4} \sin 2\theta \,d\theta\\ \amp =\amp 7\cdot\frac{3^4}{8}\left(-\cos 2\theta \bigg|_0^{\pi/2}\right)\\ \amp =\amp 7\cdot\frac{3^4}{8}\left(-( \cos \pi - \cos 0)\right)\\ \amp =\amp 141.75 \end{array}
